1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,860
A fern pot in the college corridor.

2
00:00:02,860 --> 00:00:04,602
A horsetail by a stream.

3
00:00:04,602 --> 00:00:06,813
A tiny fern floating on a pond.

4
00:00:07,213 --> 00:00:08,973
All three are pteridophytes.

5
00:00:08,973 --> 00:00:12,008
And none of them ever
makes a flower or a seed.

6
00:00:12,458 --> 00:00:16,942
Diya turned over a leaf of the corridor
fern and found rows of brown dots

7
00:00:16,942 --> 00:00:17,842
underneath.

8
00:00:17,781 --> 00:00:19,887
She asked: is the plant sick?

9
00:00:20,237 --> 00:00:21,445
It is not sick.

10
00:00:21,445 --> 00:00:23,502
Those dots are full of spores.

11
00:00:23,502 --> 00:00:25,412
That is how a fern spreads.

12
00:00:25,912 --> 00:00:30,250
By the end, you will know the body
of a pteridophyte, how it reproduces,

13
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:31,644
and its four classes.

14
00:00:32,294 --> 00:00:34,320
So what is new in pteridophytes?

15
00:00:34,670 --> 00:00:39,155
They were the first land plants with
vascular tissue: xylem and phloem.

16
00:00:39,155 --> 00:00:40,747
Xylem carries water up.

17
00:00:40,747 --> 00:00:42,534
Phloem carries food around.

18
00:00:42,534 --> 00:00:44,427
Like pipes inside the plant.

19
00:00:44,877 --> 00:00:46,919
The main plant is the sporophyte.

20
00:00:46,919 --> 00:00:50,001
It has a true root, a
true stem and true leaves.

21
00:00:50,451 --> 00:00:53,260
Most grow in cool, damp, shady places.

22
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:58,005
People use them as medicine, to hold
soil together, and as ornamental pot

23
00:00:58,005 --> 00:00:58,905
plants.

24
00:00:59,324 --> 00:01:02,677
Now let's zoom into Diya's
fern, one level at a time.

25
00:01:03,027 --> 00:01:07,772
The fern has a stem under the soil with
roots, and big leaves called fronds.

26
00:01:07,772 --> 00:01:09,848
Young fronds come out rolled up.

27
00:01:10,348 --> 00:01:12,159
Zoom in under one leaflet.

28
00:01:12,159 --> 00:01:15,038
The brown dots sit in
rows beside the vein.

29
00:01:15,538 --> 00:01:16,520
Zoom again.

30
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:21,077
Each dot is a cluster of sporangia,
tiny cases that hold spores.

31
00:01:21,577 --> 00:01:23,500
Zoom into one sporangium.

32
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:28,207
Inside, spore mother cells divide
by meiosis and make the spores.

33
00:01:28,857 --> 00:01:30,610
What happens to a spore?

34
00:01:30,610 --> 00:01:32,362
Watch it in five stages.

35
00:01:32,712 --> 00:01:33,612
Stage one.

36
00:01:33,598 --> 00:01:38,428
The spore lands on damp soil and
grows into a prothallus: a tiny, flat,

37
00:01:38,428 --> 00:01:40,843
green plant that lives on its own.

38
00:01:41,593 --> 00:01:42,493
Stage two.

39
00:01:42,485 --> 00:01:47,735
The prothallus makes the sex organs:
antheridia, the male ones, and archegonia,

40
00:01:47,735 --> 00:01:48,996
the female ones.

41
00:01:49,746 --> 00:01:50,744
Stage three.

42
00:01:50,744 --> 00:01:55,495
Male gametes, called antherozoids,
swim through water to the mouth of the

43
00:01:55,495 --> 00:01:56,431
archegonium.

44
00:01:57,181 --> 00:01:58,099
Stage four.

45
00:01:58,099 --> 00:02:00,981
One fuses with the egg and makes a zygote.

46
00:02:01,731 --> 00:02:02,644
Stage five.

47
00:02:02,644 --> 00:02:07,447
The zygote grows into a new fern,
the sporophyte, the big plant we see.

48
00:02:08,197 --> 00:02:12,850
The prothallus needs cool, damp
shade, and fertilisation needs water.

49
00:02:12,850 --> 00:02:16,241
So pteridophytes can spread
only in narrow regions.

50
00:02:16,891 --> 00:02:17,836
Remember this.

51
00:02:17,836 --> 00:02:21,103
In a pteridophyte, the plant
you see is the sporophyte.

52
00:02:21,703 --> 00:02:24,904
Not every pteridophyte makes
the same kind of spore.

53
00:02:25,254 --> 00:02:26,744
Most are homosporous.

54
00:02:26,744 --> 00:02:28,655
All their spores are alike.

55
00:02:29,105 --> 00:02:32,948
Some, like Selaginella and
Salvinia, are heterosporous.

56
00:02:32,948 --> 00:02:36,261
They make large megaspores
and small microspores.

57
00:02:36,711 --> 00:02:41,188
A megaspore grows into a female
gametophyte, and a microspore into a male

58
00:02:41,188 --> 00:02:42,088
one.

59
00:02:41,687 --> 00:02:46,321
The female one stays on the parent
plant, and the young embryo grows inside

60
00:02:46,321 --> 00:02:47,221
it.

61
00:02:47,370 --> 00:02:49,724
This is a first step towards the seed.

62
00:02:49,724 --> 00:02:52,024
It was an important step in evolution.

63
00:02:52,674 --> 00:02:53,648
Your turn.

64
00:02:53,648 --> 00:02:57,720
Selaginella makes two kinds
of spores, large and small.

65
00:02:57,720 --> 00:02:59,823
Homosporous or heterosporous?

66
00:02:59,823 --> 00:03:01,255
Pause and decide.

67
00:03:04,455 --> 00:03:05,472
Heterosporous!

68
00:03:05,472 --> 00:03:07,063
Hetero means different.

69
00:03:07,063 --> 00:03:09,128
Two different kinds of spores.

70
00:03:09,778 --> 00:03:12,027
Leaves come in two sizes too.

71
00:03:12,027 --> 00:03:15,313
Selaginella has small
leaves called microphylls.

72
00:03:15,313 --> 00:03:18,326
Ferns have large leaves
called macrophylls.

73
00:03:18,676 --> 00:03:23,308
In Selaginella and Equisetum, leaf
like sporophylls pack together into

74
00:03:23,308 --> 00:03:25,826
a compact cone called a strobilus.

75
00:03:26,426 --> 00:03:29,180
Pteridophytes are sorted
into four classes.

76
00:03:29,530 --> 00:03:31,558
Psilopsida, like Psilotum.

77
00:03:31,558 --> 00:03:34,660
Lycopsida, like
Selaginella and Lycopodium.

78
00:03:35,110 --> 00:03:38,028
Sphenopsida, like
Equisetum, the horsetail.

79
00:03:38,028 --> 00:03:42,344
And Pteropsida, the ferns, like
Dryopteris, Pteris and Adiantum.

80
00:03:42,994 --> 00:03:44,583
Now, a worked example.

81
00:03:44,583 --> 00:03:46,756
Place six plants in their classes.

82
00:03:47,106 --> 00:03:49,052
Psilotum goes to Psilopsida.

83
00:03:49,502 --> 00:03:52,722
Selaginella and Lycopodium
go to Lycopsida.

84
00:03:53,172 --> 00:03:55,434
Equisetum goes to Sphenopsida.

85
00:03:55,884 --> 00:03:59,918
Dryopteris and Adiantum are
ferns, so they go to Pteropsida.

86
00:04:00,568 --> 00:04:02,034
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:04:02,384 --> 00:04:06,998
Many students think the main plant is
the gametophyte, just like in mosses.

88
00:04:07,398 --> 00:04:08,298
Not here.

89
00:04:08,204 --> 00:04:11,528
In pteridophytes, the main
plant is the sporophyte.

90
00:04:11,528 --> 00:04:14,351
The gametophyte is only
the tiny prothallus.

91
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:16,403
Where is this asked?

92
00:04:16,403 --> 00:04:20,469
As assertion and reason, and as
match the class with its example.

93
00:04:21,069 --> 00:04:22,340
Exam level now.

94
00:04:22,340 --> 00:04:24,827
Read the assertion and the reason.

95
00:04:24,827 --> 00:04:26,152
Pause and solve.

96
00:04:31,552 --> 00:04:32,831
The answer is A.

97
00:04:32,831 --> 00:04:36,339
Both are true, and the reason
explains the assertion.

98
00:04:36,839 --> 00:04:41,573
The prothallus needs cool, damp, shady
places, and the male gametes need

99
00:04:41,573 --> 00:04:42,719
water to swim.

100
00:04:43,219 --> 00:04:48,095
That is exactly why living pteridophytes
are found only in narrow regions.

101
00:04:48,695 --> 00:04:50,154
Here is your recap.

102
00:04:50,154 --> 00:04:51,653
Screenshot this card.

103
00:04:54,853 --> 00:04:57,851
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

104
00:04:58,251 --> 00:04:59,151
One.

105
00:04:58,739 --> 00:05:01,504
What are the brown dots under a fern leaf?

106
00:05:02,404 --> 00:05:03,304
Two.

107
00:05:02,945 --> 00:05:04,577
What is a prothallus?

108
00:05:05,477 --> 00:05:06,377
Three.

109
00:05:06,137 --> 00:05:08,671
Name two heterosporous pteridophytes.

110
00:05:09,771 --> 00:05:13,060
Next time: a pine cone
picked up on a hill trip.

111
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:14,892
Where are its seeds hiding?

112
00:05:14,892 --> 00:05:16,725
We find out in gymnosperms.
